ed grounds set against a claim that there were no grounds) shows why the meaning does not follow: it MEETS, even when it closes with a bare assertion such as \"It fits the link\". Then for each judge\\'s grade: AGREE when it matches the right grade, DISAGREE when it does not.\\n'"," +'RULE E-VOID (a listed premise ruled VOID). A premise is VOID only when its charge says the opponent\\'s quoted words state something they do not state (a negation, a universal, or another claim beyond the quote). The charge is the premise\\'s own words before its quote (for example \"The inference that ...\"), read against the words of that quote. Set aside only a clause saying what the premise props (\"which props ...\") or a sentence naming the objection it serves (\"The objection it props is ...\"); those are not part of the charge. AGREE with the VOID ruling when the charge goes beyond its quote; DISAGREE when it does not.\\n'"," +'RULE E-COL (a collision line: FORCED, or LINK BROKEN). THE WITNESS LEDGER (pkgWLDDS), when an item gives it, is the system\\'s record of the rounds, the same one the judges were shown: a witness NAMED IN AN ANSWER was answered by name in the round shown, so a line that rests on that witness being unanswered or left unanswered is wrong on that ground (DISAGREE, and say so), though whether that answer meets the meaning pressed is still read from its quoted words; a witness NOT NAMED was named in no later round of the answering side; NAMED ONLY IN OTHER TEXT decides neither. A LINK BROKEN line given with THE SAME JUDGE\\'S FORCED LINE on that collision is read with it as one ruling: the words that FORCED line quotes (its MEANS and its ANSWER) are the quotes of the break, so do not DISAGREE because the LINK BROKEN line itself quotes no answer words; judge the break by those quoted words, under the rest of this rule (pkgECP, either side alike). A link is broken only when the answer\\'s own quoted words cannot agree with the meaning pressed, as the line quotes